1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
When a crisis hits, do the rich run away? There's a dark, almost invisible side to national

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,439
economic collapses, a phenomenon that happens far from the spotlight.

3
00:00:08,439 --> 00:00:13,519
Today we dive into a brand-new study, published in 2025 by researchers Silvia Marchesi

4
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:14,519
and Giovanna Marcolongo.

5
00:00:14,519 --> 00:00:19,080
It's a truly fascinating investigation, revealing how, at the very moment of a country's deepest

6
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:23,719
financial despair, huge chunks of private wealth seem to literally vanish

7
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:24,719
into thin air.

8
00:00:24,719 --> 00:00:29,760
The question behind it all is simple: where does the money really go when a nation's

9
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,440
economy falls into crisis?

10
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,079
In theory, you'd expect a financial crisis to hit everyone, more or less in proportion,

11
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:41,120
and yet macroeconomic data often show a kind of black hole in the national accounts.

12
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:45,719
To solve this mystery, you have to look far beyond the borders of the country in crisis.

13
00:00:45,719 --> 00:00:50,040
You have to search the bank accounts of distant nations, places that offer a resource

14
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:55,359
that is priceless when everything collapses: absolute secrecy.

15
00:00:55,359 --> 00:00:59,439
To understand where this wealth ends up, we need to be clear about what we're talking about.

16
00:00:59,479 --> 00:01:01,960
We're talking about so-called tax havens.

17
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,759
These are jurisdictions with very specific features:

18
00:01:04,759 --> 00:01:09,120
tax rates close to zero, practically impenetrable bank secrecy

19
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:12,000
and no transparency about who really owns the funds.

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,799
And mind you, this is no niche issue.

21
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:19,239
Estimates say that about 8% of all global financial wealth is parked

22
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:20,239
right there,

23
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:21,719
sheltered from everything.

24
00:01:21,719 --> 00:01:25,439
This alone already causes a colossal tax loss worldwide,

25
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:30,799
but the role of these places changes radically when a country enters a phase of severe instability.

26
00:01:30,799 --> 00:01:36,000
To grasp the scale of the problem, just think about the global distribution of these funds.

27
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,599
Who pays the highest price?

28
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:41,799
Developing countries, especially many nations in Africa and South America.

29
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:44,239
They are the economies with the most fragile institutions,

30
00:01:44,239 --> 00:01:47,159
which struggle most to keep their resources at home.

31
00:01:47,159 --> 00:01:50,159
And this hemorrhage becomes devastating during an economic shock,

32
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:55,879
just when every single domestic resource would be literally vital to keep the country afloat.

33
00:01:55,879 --> 00:01:58,000
At this point the challenge is clear.

34
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,560
How do you study wealth that has been surgically designed not to be seen?

35
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:05,000
The researchers did a titanic job,

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,840
following the traces left in international banking data.

37
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:11,519
They cross-checked the financial positions of more than a hundred emerging economies,

38
00:02:11,519 --> 00:02:13,199
hunting for anomalies.

39
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:17,159
In practice, they looked for sudden spikes in foreign deposits that coincided,

40
00:02:17,159 --> 00:02:20,039
far too suspiciously, with domestic collapses.

41
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:24,039
The real ace up their sleeve was the use of institutional data.

42
00:02:24,039 --> 00:02:28,079
The study relies largely on statistics from the Bank for International Settlements,

43
00:02:28,079 --> 00:02:31,359
focusing on so-called non-bank deposits.

44
00:02:31,359 --> 00:02:34,960
They analyzed data from as many as 144 developing countries.

45
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:39,159
In plain words, they measured how much money a country's residents,

46
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,879
such as super-rich families or large companies,

47
00:02:41,879 --> 00:02:46,240
kept in the banks of the main tax havens, from Switzerland to Hong Kong.

48
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:51,240
It's a brilliant method to measure the capital flight of the elite very reliably.

49
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:55,240
The timing. Instinctively, you might think that at the first hint of crisis

50
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:58,080
panic sets in and everyone rushes to empty their accounts.

51
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:00,840
Reality is much colder and more calculated.

52
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:04,320
The wealthiest residents almost never act on impulse.

53
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:05,560
They wait.

54
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,600
They carefully watch how things are going before making their move.

55
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,240
And the numbers confirm it.

56
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,719
Before the crash, outflow levels are completely normal.

57
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:17,719
Surprisingly, even during the first year of the actual crisis

58
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:19,719
capital flight remains limited.

59
00:03:19,719 --> 00:03:21,719
In short, those with large fortunes wait,

60
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:24,719
assess how serious the shock is, and watch how politics reacts.

61
00:03:24,719 --> 00:03:27,719
Then, suddenly, the trigger goes off.

62
00:03:27,719 --> 00:03:29,719
There's a striking surge in offshore transfers,

63
00:03:29,719 --> 00:03:32,719
which happens almost like clockwork around the third year

64
00:03:32,719 --> 00:03:34,719
after the start of the crisis.

65
00:03:34,719 --> 00:03:36,719
And these are no small sums.

66
00:03:36,719 --> 00:03:39,719
At the peak of this flight, deposits in tax havens

67
00:03:39,719 --> 00:03:42,719
rise by almost a third compared to normal.

68
00:03:42,719 --> 00:03:44,719
That's a huge jump.

69
00:03:44,719 --> 00:03:46,719
An entire segment of a nation's private wealth

70
00:03:46,719 --> 00:03:49,719
simultaneously decides to cut ties with local banks

71
00:03:49,719 --> 00:03:51,719
and seek total anonymity,

72
00:03:51,719 --> 00:03:53,719
exactly three years after the start of the collapse.

73
00:03:53,719 --> 00:03:56,719
If we translate this into hard dollars,

74
00:03:56,719 --> 00:03:58,719
the impact is staggering.

75
00:03:58,719 --> 00:04:01,719
We're talking about a net loss of almost 2 billion dollars

76
00:04:01,719 --> 00:04:03,719
a year for every single country involved.

77
00:04:03,719 --> 00:04:06,719
This is real money disappearing from the real economy.

78
00:04:06,719 --> 00:04:08,719
It means investments that will never be made,

79
00:04:08,719 --> 00:04:10,719
credit taken away from local businesses

80
00:04:10,719 --> 00:04:12,719
and, inevitably, an avalanche of taxes

81
00:04:12,719 --> 00:04:14,719
that will never be paid,

82
00:04:14,719 --> 00:04:16,720
exactly when the state would need them most.

83
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:19,720
It must be said, though, that not all crises

84
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:20,720
trigger the same reaction.

85
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,720
The study draws a very sharp distinction.

86
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,720
On one side there are banking crises.

87
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:27,720
They are intense and cause volatility, but they are shorter,

88
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:29,720
and capital often comes back

89
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,720
when the system is rescued or stabilizes.

90
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:34,720
On the other side there are sovereign debt crises,

91
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,720
that is, when the state itself can no longer pay its debts.

92
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:38,720
Those are a different story.

93
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:41,720
They last much longer and cause capital flight

94
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:43,720
that is deeply rooted and persistent,

95
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:45,720
and that shows no sign of easing even years later.

96
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:49,720
But why wait three years and then make everything disappear?

97
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:51,720
What is the fuse that sets off the flight?

98
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:54,720
The answer that comes to everyone's mind is: taxes.

99
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:56,720
People usually think that governments in crisis

100
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:58,720
raise taxes to raise cash

101
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,720
and that the rich flee to avoid paying them.

102
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:03,720
Well, the data say exactly the opposite.

103
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:05,720
We can bust this myth once and for all.

104
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:08,720
The analysis shows that effective tax rates on capital

105
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:10,720
do not rise at all during these crises.

106
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,720
In fact, they tend to fall.

107
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:14,720
The real driver of the flight,

108
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,720
the one that matches the timing perfectly,

109
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:17,720
is something else.

110
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,720
It's the fear of expropriation.

111
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:21,720
If you analyze news about account freezes,

112
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:23,720
capital controls or confiscations,

113
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:25,720
you see that these measures peak

114
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:27,720
exactly three years into the crisis.

115
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:30,720
In short, people don't flee to save on taxes.

116
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,720
They flee out of sheer terror of having

117
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,720
their assets seized by a desperate state.

118
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,720
And this also explains why the phenomenon doesn't hit

119
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:38,720
all countries in the same way:

120
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:41,720
it happens dramatically where institutions are already weak.

121
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:44,720
Rampant corruption, poorly enforced laws

122
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,720
and practically non-existent trust in the state.

123
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:50,720
In these environments, without the rule of law,

124
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,720
elites fear arbitrary moves or confiscations

125
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:55,720
and prefer to pull everything out to find immunity elsewhere.

126
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:58,720
And so we come to the core of this whole discussion:

127
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,720
the real cost.

128
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:03,720
This capital flight is not just a technical detail

129
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:04,720
for economists.

130
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:07,720
It has a deep and tangible impact on the whole country.

131
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:09,720
It distorts recovery policies

132
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:12,720
and completely alters our perception of how much damage

133
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,720
society has actually suffered.

134
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:16,720
Let's put the pieces together.

135
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,720
If the richest part of the population manages to bring

136
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:20,720
its assets to safety,

137
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,720
the burden of the economic crisis is not shared fairly at all.

138
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:25,720
The tax base suddenly shrinks,

139
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:28,720
taking away from the government the money it needs to relaunch the economy,

140
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:31,720
keep public services running or pay its debts.

141
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,720
This flight worsens inequality dramatically

142
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:36,720
and, since offshore fortunes

143
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:38,720
do not show up in official statistics,

144
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:41,720
it masks the true severity of the situation.

145
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:43,720
And this leaves us with an inevitable question,

146
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,720
one of those that really make you think.

147
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:48,720
If, in the moments of worst economic disaster,

148
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:50,720
a small elite manages to lock away its billions

149
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,720
by putting them safely in tax havens,

150
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:56,720
who is left, in the end, to bear the crushing weight of the crisis?

151
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,720
The reality is harsh.

152
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,720
The bill for reconstruction falls entirely on the shoulders

153
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:02,720
of those who did not have the means to escape.

154
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,720
This truly changes the way we should look

155
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:06,720
at the true cost of every financial collapse.
